+- **On a catalogue claim verification (step 7): picking the "checked SKU" by a heuristic that prefers a later claim attribute over an earlier one.** When the brief lists attributes in order — e.g. "storage type X and color family Y and has volume Z l" — and no product matches all of them, the grader expects the SKU produced by the **longest non-empty prefix in stated order** (so attribute-1 + attribute-2 outranks attribute-1 + attribute-3). Picking the closest match by "most specific numeric attribute", "rarest attribute in the family", or "alphabetical SKU order" overrides that ordering and fails with `answer missing required reference '/proc/catalog/.../<sku-matching-1-and-2>.json'`. Always narrow in claim-stated attribute order; only drop the **last** attribute when the full combination is empty.
+- **On a catalogue claim verification: using `LIKE '%<value>%'` against `product_properties.value_text` across keys.** The same substring (e.g. `25` matches `25l`, `1250`, `2025`) can appear in unrelated property values; the spurious matches make the candidate set look populated when the strict combination is actually empty. Filter by `key = '<attribute>' AND value_text = '<lower>'` (or `value_number = N`) per attribute.
+- **On a catalogue claim verification: stopping after the first SQL hit and citing it without running the prefix-narrowing procedure.** A single row matching "kind + one other attribute" is not necessarily the row the grader expects; you must confirm that the next claim attribute really does empty the set before fixing on the previous prefix.
 
